// ScaleHistory — `docs/optimization-log.md`. The trend IS the deliverable.
//
// This instrument renders no verdict, so it needs a reference point, and the reference point is the
// log: THE LAST ROW OF EACH TABLE IS THE PREVIOUS DATAPOINT. Every run reads it and reports the
// delta from it, so you still see exactly what moved — without anything passing or failing. There is
// no separate baseline file, and there is nothing to "accept".
//
// WHY A RUN DOES NOT RECORD ITSELF, and `--note=<text>` does.
//
// scale-test is run dozens of times a day against dirty trees, half-finished experiments and
// abandoned branches. A log that recorded every one of them would be mostly noise — which is to say,
// a log nobody reads and nobody can trust. `--note` is the moment a human says "this run is a
// datapoint worth keeping, and here is what changed".
//
// AND WHEN IT IS GIVEN, THE ROW IS WRITTEN — even if not one allocation moved. The operator asked
// for the datapoint; declining it because the instrument could not see what was interesting about it
// is the tool overruling the only party who knows why the run was made. The report still says
// plainly that the memory did not move.
//
// AND THE NOTE IS THE POINT, not a formality. The instrument can see precisely WHAT moved and can
// never see WHY. Six months from now "allocations fell 8% at rung 5" is worth almost nothing without
// the sentence naming the change that did it — and that sentence exists only in the head of whoever
// ran the command, at the moment they ran it. So it is demanded there, rather than left as a
// placeholder that would be committed unfilled.
//
// TWO TABLES, each read DOWNWARDS: one row per recorded change, one column per RUNG.
//
//   allocations  Exact and bit-for-bit reproducible for one checkout. Across two, it carries a
//                CONSTANT offset — so its SHAPE survives and its constant term does not.
//   bytes        Exact and bit-for-bit reproducible for one checkout, and NOT comparable across two.
//
// ===========================================================================================
// WHY EVERY ROW RECORDS WHERE IT WAS MINTED, AND WHAT EACH COLUMN CAN STILL CARRY WITHOUT IT
// ===========================================================================================
//
// A compile ALLOCATES THE PATH STRINGS IT IS WORKING WITH, so the BYTE volume of a compile carries a
// term proportional to the LENGTH of those paths. It is not small and it is not flat. There are TWO of
// them, they come from two different places, and a key that names only one licenses exactly the
// subtraction this file exists to refuse:
//
//   1. THE COMPILER'S OWN EXECUTABLE PATH. `locateStdlibDir` walks up from it and derives the absolute
//      path of every stdlib source file from what it finds. MEASURED, one binary, two real checkouts
//      44 characters apart, one working directory each, six rungs:
//
//        bytes        +92,576 / +102,740 / +123,068 / +163,724 / +245,036 / +407,660
//        allocations  0 / 0 / 0 / 0 / 0 / 0
//
//   2. THE WORKING DIRECTORY. The rung sources are handed to the compiler as a RELATIVE path on purpose
//      (see `CorpusRelativeRoot`), and that does NOT keep the checkout out of the byte column, because
//      the compiler resolves each source path against `Directory.currentPath()` itself —
//      `Queries.isStdlibSourceFile` does it PER FILE, per compile. MEASURED, one binary at ONE
//      executable path, two working directories 44 characters apart, six rungs:
//
//        bytes        +34,515 / +39,960 / +50,850 / +72,630 / +116,190 / +203,310
//        allocations  0 / 0 / 0 / 0 / 0 / 0
//
// ⇒ BOTH are in the key, because either one alone answers "comparable" for a pair that is not. (2) was
// found in review, by running one binary from a second directory: the guard said `comparable` and the
// report printed a confident +203,310 at rung 5 — the very fiction it had just been built to withhold.
//
// ⚠ AND THE `allocations 0` ON BOTH ROWS ABOVE IS NOT THE GENERAL RESULT IT WAS READ AS. Both varied
// the LENGTH of a path, and the allocation column happens not to see that. It sees something else, and
// the two experiments could not have found it — see the third measurement below, which was run because
// this file's own claim of `path-independent` cost an implementer most of a session.
//
// So the two columns are NOT the same kind of number across checkouts, they are BOTH spoiled by a
// mismatched pair, and the whole mechanism here is the difference in HOW:
//
//   - BYTES CARRY A TERM THAT GROWS WITH THE PROGRAM. It sits in `phase:load` (flat),
//     `phase:signatures` and `phase:parse` (both rising with it), so it is not a constant offset a
//     reader could subtract — it ran ~2,100 bytes per character at rung 0 to ~9,300 at rung 5. There is
//     nothing left of a byte A/B across two checkouts, so the delta is WITHHELD.
//   - ALLOCATIONS CARRY A CONSTANT. The same number at every rung — which is exactly what a startup
//     cost looks like, and the reason the delta is still PRINTED: a flat offset cancels out of every
//     rung-over-rung RATIO and out of any delta that GROWS per rung, so the SHAPE of an allocation A/B
//     across two checkouts stands. Its CONSTANT TERM does not, and a flat per-rung delta between two
//     checkouts is therefore unattributable. MEASURED, ONE binary — built twice, in two worktrees of
//     one commit, and confirmed byte-identical by hash — run at four real checkout paths, six rungs:
//
//       45 chars, 7 path components   778,291 … 18,109,206   a real worktree, AND a synthetic path of
//                                                            the same length and depth: identical cells
//       46 chars, 5 path components   778,147 … 18,109,062
//       45 chars, 3 path components   778,018 … 18,108,933
//       14 chars, 3 path components   778,018 … 18,108,933   identical to the 45-character row above it
//
//     ⇒ +273 at all six rungs between two paths of the SAME LENGTH, and 0 at all six between paths 31
//     characters apart. The offset is flat in every pair and lands in `phase:parse` alone.
//     ⚠ SO THE RULE IS NOT "EQUALISE THE PATH LENGTH". What this column tracks is measurably not the
//     character count, and this file does not claim to know what it IS — which costs nothing, because
//     the key below needs no such knowledge.
//
// ⇒ A byte delta between two rows minted at different paths is a CONFIDENT FICTION, and it has been
// read as a per-function regression four times by two agents in one session — once as a sign reversal.
// A CONSTANT allocation delta between them is the same fiction one column over, and it was nearly filed
// as a rung's cost twice more. This file therefore records the minting paths in the row and
// `byteComparability` decides; the runner WITHHOLDS the byte delta and prints the allocation delta under
// the caveat that its constant term is not attributable. Nothing is normalised: the counts stay exactly
// what was measured, and a subtraction that cannot be honest is simply not performed.
//
// THE PATHS THEMSELVES, NOT THEIR LENGTHS, and the third measurement above turned that from the
// stronger key into the only correct one. For BYTES, length would do under an assumption (that the
// executable's own file NAME is a build constant, so equal total length implies equal DIRECTORY length,
// which is the component that multiplies). For ALLOCATIONS it would be simply WRONG: two 45-character
// paths differ by 273. The whole string needs no assumption in either column — equal paths allocate
// equally, full stop — and it is also the thing a human reading the log downwards actually wants, since
// "this row came from a worktree" is the sentence that explains a step in the column.
//
// ⚠ A SYMLINK OR JUNCTION IS NOT A DIFFERENT PATH. Windows resolves one before the process sees its own
// executable path, so an A/B run through two junctions of different lengths pointing at one tree
// measures NOTHING and reads as proof of path-independence. Every reading above comes from real
// directories. (`Process.executablePath()` is what is recorded, so whatever the OS reports is what the
// compile saw — which is the correct thing to compare either way.)
//
// AND NOTHING ELSE — no time, and no fitted exponents. There WAS a third table of per-curve growth
// exponents, `time / allocations`, and it is gone. Time is machine-dependent, so a dated column of it
// compares a loaded box in July against an idle one in August and concludes nothing. And an exponent
// added nothing to a ladder that DOUBLES: rung over rung, x2.00 is linear and x4.00 is quadratic, and
// that is a division a reader can do on the numbers already in these tables. The row that exists
// carries the raw counts, which is the thing that cannot go out of date.
//
// (The exponents table's rows are still in the document, under a heading that says they are CLOSED.
// Deleting recorded history to tidy up a format change would be the worst of both: the numbers were
// honestly measured, and they are simply no longer extended.)
//
// A new row goes immediately BEFORE the marker that closes its table. Anchoring on a marker rather
// than appending to the end of the file is what lets the document keep prose after the tables — the
// commentary explaining WHY a number moved, which is the whole reason this is markdown and not a TSV.

export let OptimizationLogRelativePath = "docs/optimization-log.md"

let AllocationsTableMarker = "<!-- scale-history:allocations -->"
let BytesTableMarker = "<!-- scale-history:bytes -->"

// The CPU table is the one column here that is not bit-reproducible (see PhaseMetric), so its
// section in the log carries the measured noise band and the rule that a movement inside it is
// not a datapoint. It is recorded anyway, because it is the ONLY column that can see a cost
// which allocates nothing — the class of defect the other two are structurally blind to.
let CpuTableMarker = "<!-- scale-history:cpu -->"

// What both markers above begin with, and the ONE thing every written cell is screened against — see
// `assertCellSafe`. A cell is written verbatim into the row inserted above each marker in turn, so a
// cell that carried a marker's own text would forge one: the row for the first table would contain an
// anchor the second table's insert then found. Screening the shared PREFIX refuses that whole family —
// including any marker a later table adds — rather than two literals a third could silently escape.
// (`insertRowBefore` is the backstop, and it reads the document rather than the cell.)
let TableMarkerPrefix = "<!-- scale-history:"

// The three fixed leading columns every table carries. The rest are rungs, which is what
// `LeadingColumns` is subtracted to find.
//
// EACH COLUMN'S HEADING, ITS INDEX AND THE COUNT ARE ONE FACT, so they are written to derive from each
// other rather than to agree by hand. The count is the last index plus one, and `assertLeadingHeadings`
// CHECKS the document's own header row against the headings at those indices — this went from two to
// three when `minted at` landed, and a reader that had taken the new width but kept the old indices
// would have read the note as a path and rung 0's count as the note, silently, off a log whose header
// says otherwise.
let DateColumnHeading = "date"
let MintedAtColumnHeading = "minted at"
let NoteColumnHeading = "change"

let DateColumnIndex = 0
let MintedAtColumnIndex = 1
let NoteColumnIndex = 2
let LeadingColumns = NoteColumnIndex + 1

// WHERE A ROW WAS MEASURED — BOTH paths whose length reaches its byte column: the compiler that produced
// it, and the corpus it compiled. The one thing that decides whether two rows' BYTE columns may be
// subtracted; see this file's header for the two measurements.
//
// A UNION, because "this row does not say" is a real third answer and not a path. Every row written
// before the column existed carries `MintedAtUnrecorded`, and those rows' byte deltas are as
// unverifiable as a mismatched pair's — which is a different claim from "they match", and an empty
// string would have said neither.
export union MintedAt
	unrecorded
	path(text String)
end 'MintedAt'

// THE CELL, COMPOSED IN ONE PLACE — so the string a row is WRITTEN with and the string a later run is
// COMPARED against cannot be assembled differently. Both halves are in it because the byte column
// carries a term proportional to each (see the header); comparison stays whole-string equality, which
// needs no assumption about which half moved.
export function mintedAtCell(compilerPath String, corpusRoot String) returns String
	return "{compilerPath} (corpus {corpusRoot})"
end 'mintedAtCell'

// What a row minted before this column existed carries. Deliberately not a path and not empty: the
// document's own idiom for a cell with no value is an italic parenthetical (`*(baseline)*` in the
// oldest note), and no absolute path can collide with it.
let MintedAtUnrecorded = "*(unrecorded)*"

// The counts as the tables print them. Signed because a delta is, and because `groupDigits` walks a
// rendered number that may carry a minus sign.
export typealias Delta = int(i64.min to i64.max)

typealias CivilPart = int(0 to u64.max)
typealias DayCount = int(0 to u64.max)
typealias GroupIndex = int(0 to u64.max)

// A column of one of the log's markdown tables: 0 is `date`, 1 is `minted at`, 2 is `change`, and the
// rest are rungs.
typealias ColumnIndex = int(0 to u64.max)

let SecondsPerDay = 86400 as UnixSeconds
let DigitGroupSize = 3 as GroupIndex

// EVERYTHING THE LOG HAS TO SAY ABOUT THE RUN BEFORE THIS ONE — which, now that the exponents table
// is closed, is exactly its memory.
//
// This was a `ScaleTrend` struct wrapping this union plus two maps of per-curve exponents. With the
// exponents gone it would have been a struct with one field, which is a wrapper that makes every
// caller write `.memory` to say nothing.
//
// A union rather than an empty-array sentinel, because here the distinction is real: "the log has no
// rows yet" is a first datapoint, and "the log's last row was all zeroes" would be a broken compiler.
// An empty array would say both.
export union LoggedMemory
	absent
	present(date String, mintedAt MintedAt, note String, allocs AllocCountArray, bytes ByteCountArray)

	// How many rungs the memory tables are keyed by. A run with a different ladder length cannot be
	// appended to them without going ragged — see `assertRecordable`.
	export function loggedRungCount() returns RungCount throws ScaleError
		match self 'm'
			absent then throw ScaleError.reason("the log has no rows, so it pins no ladder length")
			present(_, _, _, allocs, _) then return allocs.count() as RungCount
		end 'm'
	end 'loggedRungCount'
end 'LoggedMemory'

// ===========================================================================================
// Were this run and the logged row minted at the SAME path?
// ===========================================================================================

// THE ONE DECISION THIS PROVENANCE EXISTS TO MAKE — asked once, and read by BOTH columns, because both
// are spoiled by a mismatched pair and only the consequence differs (see this file's header). `withheld`
// carries the sentence the report prints, because the answer is only useful with the reason attached — a
// bare `false` in front of a blank column is the same silence the confident wrong number replaced.
//
// IT KEEPS THE BYTE COLUMN'S NAME because the byte column is the only thing it REFUSES. The allocation
// column is printed either way; what the same answer buys there is the caveat under the table, and a
// second union to say "the paths differ" a second time is the duplication this instrument is named for.
//
// ⚠ IT IS NOT A VERDICT ABOUT THE COMPILER. The run still exits 0, still reports every count it
// measured, and still records the row if it was asked to. What is refused is one SUBTRACTION, on the
// ground that its two operands were not measured against the same yardstick.
export union ByteDelta
	comparable
	withheld(why String)
end 'ByteDelta'

// Compare the whole path, for the reason the header gives: equal paths allocate equally, and no
// assumption about which PART of the path multiplies has to hold for that to be true — an assumption
// the allocation column has since been measured to break.
export function byteComparability(trend LoggedMemory, mintedAt String) returns ByteDelta
	match trend 'm'
		absent then return ByteDelta.comparable
		present(date, logged, _, _, _) then return byteComparabilityAgainst(logged, mintedAt: mintedAt, date: date)
	end 'm'
end 'byteComparability'

function byteComparabilityAgainst(logged MintedAt, mintedAt String, date String) returns ByteDelta
	match logged 'l'
		unrecorded then return ByteDelta.withheld("the {date} row was written before rows recorded where they were minted, so there is nothing to compare this run's path against")
		path(text) then return byteComparabilityOfPaths(text, mintedAt: mintedAt)
	end 'l'
end 'byteComparabilityAgainst'

function byteComparabilityOfPaths(logged String, mintedAt String) returns ByteDelta
	if logged == mintedAt 'samePath'
		return ByteDelta.comparable
	end 'samePath'

	return ByteDelta.withheld("that row was minted at `{logged}` ({logged.count()} characters) and this run at `{mintedAt}` ({mintedAt.count()})")
end 'byteComparabilityOfPaths'

// ===========================================================================================
// Reading the log
// ===========================================================================================

// One table of the log, as parsed. `last` is the row this run is differenced against.
//
// IT CARRIES THE TWO THINGS EVERY REFUSAL ABOUT IT HAS TO NAME — the document it was read out of and
// the marker that closes it — rather than having each of them handed back in beside it at every call.
// `lastRowOf` used to take the marker as a second argument that could only ever be the one the table
// was read with; a table that knows which table it is cannot be described as a different one.
type LogTable
	export let source as String
	export let marker as String
	export let header as StringArray
	export let last as LogTableRow

	static function create(source String, marker String, header StringArray, last LogTableRow) returns LogTable
		return Self{source: source, marker: marker, header: header, last: last}
	end 'create'
end 'LogTable'

union LogTableRow
	none
	cells(values StringArray)
end 'LogTableRow'

// Read the trend: the last row of the two memory tables, which are written together and must
// therefore describe the same run.
//
// NOTHING IS READ BACK BUT NUMBERS AND PROVENANCE. The exponents table is closed, so there is no longer
// a cell to parse into a fitted value, no `n/a` token to round-trip, and no table header whose columns
// have to be checked against the phases this compiler happens to have. Every one of those was a way for
// a document to be written in a shape it could not be read in — the class of bug that produced a
// write-only log — and they are gone because the thing they carried is gone. The `minted at` cell is
// read back because it decides whether one of the two columns may be differenced at all, which is a
// question about THIS document rather than about a curve.
export function loadScaleTrend() returns LoggedMemory throws ScaleError
	let path = try FilePath.from(OptimizationLogRelativePath) otherwise throw ScaleError.reason("'{OptimizationLogRelativePath}' is not a valid path")

	// The log is hand-written prose with machine-appended table rows. The instrument extends it and
	// never creates it: if it is missing, the likely cause is a run from the wrong directory, and
	// minting a fresh file would bury the real history rather than continue it.
	let text = try File.readText(path) otherwise throw ScaleError.reason("could not read {OptimizationLogRelativePath} — it is the trend this run is measured against, not a file the instrument creates. Run scale-test from the repo root.")

	return try parseScaleTrend(text, source: OptimizationLogRelativePath)
end 'loadScaleTrend'

// THE PARSER, SEPARATED FROM THE FILE READ — and that separation is the whole seam.
//
// ⭐ **EVERY REFUSAL BELOW WAS RUN BY NOTHING.** There is no route to a damaged trend log from a spec
// case, so around thirty throws describing exactly how this document can be hand-edited into a lie had
// never once been observed to fire — a claim, not a door. `ScaleHistorySelfTest` now proves each of them
// by handing THIS function deliberately-damaged documents, in process, on every suite run.
//
// ⚠ **AND IT IS THIS FUNCTION, NOT A SECOND ONE WRITTEN TO BE TESTABLE.** A parser built for the test
// beside the parser the tool uses is this project's signature bug — one fact written down twice, with
// the copy under test and the original unwatched. The log has exactly one parser; what the caller
// supplies is the text and its NAME.
//
// `source` is that name, and it is a parameter rather than the constant every message used to
// interpolate: a refusal raised over a fixture must not tell its reader that `docs/optimization-log.md`
// is broken when that file is fine.
export function parseScaleTrend(text String, source String) returns LoggedMemory throws ScaleError
	// SPLIT ONCE. The three markers are three anchors in ONE document, and each table used to re-split
	// the whole file to find its own.
	let lines = text.split("\n")

	let allocations = try readTable(lines, marker: AllocationsTableMarker, source: source)
	let bytes = try readTable(lines, marker: BytesTableMarker, source: source)

	// THE CPU TABLE IS READ TOO, AND ITS NUMBERS ARE NOT USED. `recordScaleRun` WRITES a row into it, so
	// leaving it unread made it the one table whose shape nothing checked — a CPU table that had gone
	// ragged, or kept a leading column the other two had dropped, would take row after row of the wrong
	// width forever and no run would ever say so. A missing check never fails.
	let cpu = try readTable(lines, marker: CpuTableMarker, source: source)

	return try readMemoryRow(allocations, bytesTable: bytes, cpuTable: cpu)
end 'parseScaleTrend'

// The table that ends at `marker`: the contiguous run of `|`-leading lines immediately above it.
//
// Bottom-up from the marker, because that is the ONE anchor the document guarantees. Searching
// top-down from a heading would tie the parser to prose the author is free to rewrite; the marker is
// there precisely so they can.
function readTable(lines StringArray, marker String, source String) returns LogTable throws ScaleError
	var markerLine = lines.count()
	for i in 0 upto lines.count() 'findMarker'
		let line = try lines.get(i) otherwise panic("readTable: i < count but lines.get({i}) failed")
		if line.trim() == marker 'found'
			markerLine = i
			break
		end 'found'
	end 'findMarker'

	if markerLine >= lines.count() 'noMarker'
		throw ScaleError.reason("{source} is missing the table marker `{marker}`. A row is inserted immediately before it; without it there is no table to extend.")
	end 'noMarker'

	// Walk up over the table's rows to its header. The block is header, separator, then zero or more
	// data rows — so the topmost `|` line is the header and the bottommost is the row we want.
	var top = markerLine
	while top > 0 'toTop'
		let line = try lines.get(top - 1) otherwise panic("readTable: top > 0 but lines.get({top - 1}) failed")
		if not line.startsWith("|") 'aboveTheTable'
			break
		end 'aboveTheTable'
		top = top - 1
	end 'toTop'

	let rowCount = markerLine - top
	if rowCount < TableHeaderLines 'noHeader'
		throw ScaleError.reason("the table above `{marker}` in {source} has no header row and separator — it must be `| {DateColumnHeading} | {MintedAtColumnHeading} | {NoteColumnHeading} | ... |` followed by `| --- | ... |`")
	end 'noHeader'

	let header = splitRow(try lines.get(top) otherwise panic("readTable: header line is inside the block"))
	try assertLeadingHeadings(header, marker: marker, source: source)
	try assertSeparatorRow(splitRow(try lines.get(top + 1) otherwise panic("readTable: the separator line is inside the block")), header: header, marker: marker, source: source)

	if rowCount == TableHeaderLines 'headerOnly'
		return LogTable.create(source, marker: marker, header: header, last: LogTableRow.none)
	end 'headerOnly'

	let lastCells = splitRow(try lines.get(markerLine - 1) otherwise panic("readTable: the last row is inside the block"))
	if lastCells.count() != header.count() 'ragged'
		throw ScaleError.reason("the last row of the table above `{marker}` in {source} has {lastCells.count()} cell(s) but its header has {header.count()} — the table is ragged, and a column read out of it would be the wrong column")
	end 'ragged'

	return LogTable.create(source, marker: marker, header: header, last: LogTableRow.cells(lastCells))
end 'readTable'

// A markdown table always opens with a heading row and a `| --- |` separator; data rows come after.
let TableHeaderLines = 2

// THE DOCUMENT AND THIS READER MUST AGREE ON WHICH COLUMN IS WHICH — read off the header row, not
// assumed from a count.
//
// Every leading cell is fetched by INDEX (`memoryRowFrom`) and the rung columns begin at
// `LeadingColumns`, so a log whose tables carry a different set of leading columns is not merely
// mis-parsed, it is mis-parsed CONFIDENTLY: with the pre-`minted at` two-column header, cell 1 reads as
// a minting path when it is the note, and rung 0's count reads as the note. That state arrives by
// ordinary means — a rebase onto a branch whose log half was not migrated, a merge that took one side of
// the document. The header row says plainly which columns the document has; this is the one place it is
// believed.
function assertLeadingHeadings(header StringArray, marker String, source String) throws ScaleError
	try assertHeading(header, index: DateColumnIndex, expected: DateColumnHeading, marker: marker, source: source)
	try assertHeading(header, index: MintedAtColumnIndex, expected: MintedAtColumnHeading, marker: marker, source: source)
	try assertHeading(header, index: NoteColumnIndex, expected: NoteColumnHeading, marker: marker, source: source)
end 'assertLeadingHeadings'

function assertHeading(header StringArray, index ColumnIndex, expected String, marker String, source String) throws ScaleError
	let heading = try cellAt(header, index: index, source: source)
	if heading != expected 'wrongColumn'
		throw ScaleError.reason("column {index} of the table above `{marker}` in {source} is headed '{heading}' and this compiler reads it as `{expected}` — the leading columns are `{DateColumnHeading}`, `{MintedAtColumnHeading}`, `{NoteColumnHeading}`, then one per rung. A row read against the wrong columns would take a note for a minting path and a rung's count for a note.")
	end 'wrongColumn'
end 'assertHeading'

// The second line of the block IS the separator — CHECKED, never assumed.
//
// `readTable` finds the header by walking UP from the marker to the topmost `|` line, so a separator
// that has been damaged (deleted, blanked, or pushed apart by a stray blank line) does not fail: it
// yields a table one line shorter, in which the first DATA row is read as the header and every row
// below it is discarded. The log then cheerfully reports "no rows yet — this run is the first
// datapoint" over a document full of history, `assertRecordable` stops pinning the ladder's width
// because it believes there is nothing to be ragged against, and a `--note` appends a row to a table
// nobody read correctly. Every one of those is silent, which is why this is a throw and not a repair.
function assertSeparatorRow(cells StringArray, header StringArray, marker String, source String) throws ScaleError
	if cells.count() != header.count() 'wrongWidth'
		throw ScaleError.reason("the separator under the header of the table above `{marker}` in {source} has {cells.count()} cell(s) and the header has {header.count()}")
	end 'wrongWidth'

	for cell in cells 'eachCell'
		if not isSeparatorCell(cell) 'notASeparator'
			throw ScaleError.reason("the second line of the table above `{marker}` in {source} is not a `| --- | ---: |` separator — its cell '{cell}' is not one. A markdown header is followed by exactly one separator, and without it the first DATA row is read as the header and every row below it is silently lost.")
		end 'notASeparator'
	end 'eachCell'
end 'assertSeparatorRow'

// `---`, `:---`, `---:`, `:---:` — a markdown alignment cell is dashes and colons, and nothing else.
function isSeparatorCell(cell String) returns bool
	if cell.isEmpty() 'empty'
		return false
	end 'empty'

	for ch in cell.toByteArray() 'eachByte'
		if ch != AsciiDash and ch != AsciiColon 'notAlignment'
			return false
		end 'notAlignment'
	end 'eachByte'

	return true
end 'isSeparatorCell'

let AsciiDash = 0x2D
let AsciiColon = 0x3A

// `| a | b | c |` -> ["a", "b", "c"]. The leading and trailing pipes each produce an empty field,
// which is what the two drops are for.
function splitRow(line String) returns StringArray
	let raw = line.split("|")
	var cells = StringArray.create()
	for i in 1 upto raw.count() - 1 'eachCell'
		cells.push((try raw.get(i) otherwise panic("splitRow: i < count-1 but get({i}) failed")).trim())
	end 'eachCell'
	return cells
end 'splitRow'

// The last row of each of the three tables, which are written together by one run and must therefore
// describe the same run. If they do not, someone hand-edited one of them, and differencing this run
// against two different pasts would be worse than not differencing it at all.
function readMemoryRow(allocations LogTable, bytesTable LogTable, cpuTable LogTable) returns LoggedMemory throws ScaleError
	match allocations.last 'a'
		none then return LoggedMemory.absent
		cells(allocCells) then return try memoryRowFrom(allocCells, bytesTable: bytesTable, cpuTable: cpuTable)
	end 'a'
end 'readMemoryRow'

// The `minted at` cell of a row. A row is written with a real path or with `MintedAtUnrecorded`, so an
// EMPTY cell is a damaged row rather than a third state — and reading it as "unrecorded" would silently
// turn a hand-edit that deleted a path into a row that merely declines to say.
function mintedAtFrom(cell String, source String) returns MintedAt throws ScaleError
	if cell.isEmpty() 'damaged'
		throw ScaleError.reason("a row in {source} has an EMPTY `{MintedAtColumnHeading}` cell — a row records the compiler path it was measured at, or `{MintedAtUnrecorded}` if it predates the column, and an empty cell is neither")
	end 'damaged'

	if cell == MintedAtUnrecorded 'predatesTheColumn'
		return MintedAt.unrecorded
	end 'predatesTheColumn'

	return MintedAt.path(cell)
end 'mintedAtFrom'

// The last data row of a table the allocations table has a counterpart in. One function for all of them:
// the message names the table by the marker every other message here identifies a table by, so a third
// table needs no third copy of it.
function lastRowOf(table LogTable) returns StringArray throws ScaleError
	match table.last 'b'
		none then throw ScaleError.reason("the allocations table has rows but the table above `{table.marker}` in {table.source} has none — all three are written together by one run, so one of them has been hand-edited")
		cells(values) then return values
	end 'b'
end 'lastRowOf'

// The last row of each table must agree on EVERY leading cell, provenance included. The minting path
// decides whether the byte column may be differenced, so the row that carries the bytes and the row that
// states where they came from being one and the same run is exactly the property being relied on.
function memoryRowFrom(allocCells StringArray, bytesTable LogTable, cpuTable LogTable) returns LoggedMemory throws ScaleError
	// All three tables were read out of one document, so any of them names it.
	let source = bytesTable.source

	let byteCells = try lastRowOf(bytesTable)
	let cpuCells = try lastRowOf(cpuTable)

	let date = try cellAt(allocCells, index: DateColumnIndex, source: source)
	let mintedAt = try cellAt(allocCells, index: MintedAtColumnIndex, source: source)
	let note = try cellAt(allocCells, index: NoteColumnIndex, source: source)

	try assertSameRun(byteCells, date: date, mintedAt: mintedAt, note: note, marker: bytesTable.marker, source: source)
	try assertSameRun(cpuCells, date: date, mintedAt: mintedAt, note: note, marker: cpuTable.marker, source: source)

	let allocs = try rungCounts(allocCells, source: source)
	let bytes = try rungCounts(byteCells, source: source)

	// The CPU row's counts are PARSED and then only counted. That is the check: the row this run's write
	// will land beneath is numeric and the same width as the other two, which is the whole of what the
	// writer assumes about it.
	let cpu = try rungCounts(cpuCells, source: source)
	if allocs.count() != bytes.count() or allocs.count() != cpu.count() 'differentLadders'
		throw ScaleError.reason("the last rows of the three tables have {allocs.count()}, {bytes.count()} and {cpu.count()} rung(s) — they measure one ladder, so one of them has been hand-edited")
	end 'differentLadders'

	return LoggedMemory.present(date, mintedAt: try mintedAtFrom(mintedAt, source: source), note: note, allocs: allocs, bytes: bytes)
end 'memoryRowFrom'

// One table's last row against the allocations table's, on the three leading cells.
//
// ⭐ **IT NAMES THE CELL THAT DIFFERS, AND SHOWS BOTH OF THAT CELL'S VALUES.** This message used to
// interpolate the date and the minting path of both rows — and never the NOTE, which is the third cell
// it COMPARES and by far the likeliest of the three to have been hand-edited. So the one failure it was
// most often raised by printed two byte-identical descriptors and told the reader they described
// different runs. (Reproduced: appending ` (hand-edited tail)` to the bytes table's note produced two
// parentheticals the reader could not tell apart, and no sight of the text that had actually moved.)
//
// EVERY differing cell is reported, not the first. One bad rebase moves all three at once, and a message
// naming one of them sends the reader back for another full sweep per remaining cell — the same argument
// `LadderIndex` reports its three directions together for.
//
// ⚠ **THE NOTES ARE 2,205–3,753 CHARACTERS in the live log, so two of them cannot be printed whole — and
// a bare head would not do either.** The failure that occurred differed in its LAST sentence, where the
// first eighty characters of the two are identical, so a head would print two indistinguishable excerpts
// and reproduce the original defect one layer down. The excerpt is therefore a window around the FIRST
// character that differs, and `cellDifferenceReport` states that rule where the reader is looking at its
// output rather than in a comment they are not reading.
function assertSameRun(cells StringArray, date String, mintedAt String, note String, marker String, source String) throws ScaleError
	var differing = StringArray.create()
	recordCellDifference(differing, heading: DateColumnHeading, logged: date, other: try cellAt(cells, index: DateColumnIndex, source: source))
	recordCellDifference(differing, heading: MintedAtColumnHeading, logged: mintedAt, other: try cellAt(cells, index: MintedAtColumnIndex, source: source))
	recordCellDifference(differing, heading: NoteColumnHeading, logged: note, other: try cellAt(cells, index: NoteColumnIndex, source: source))

	if differing.isEmpty() 'sameRun'
		return
	end 'sameRun'

	throw ScaleError.reason("the last row of the table above `{marker}` in {source} does not describe the same run as the last allocations row: {differing.count()} of its {LeadingColumns} leading cell(s) differ. All three tables are written together by one run, so one of them has been hand-edited.{cellDifferenceReport(differing)}")
end 'assertSameRun'

// Record `heading` as a difference IF the two rows disagree about it.
//
// An accumulator that is APPENDED TO rather than a String that is empty when the cells agree: the
// caller counts the differences to say how many there are, and an empty-string sentinel would make it
// re-derive "no clause" from "a clause that says nothing".
function recordCellDifference(differing StringArray, heading String, logged String, other String)
	if logged == other 'agrees'
		return
	end 'agrees'

	let at = firstDivergenceAt(logged, other: other)
	differing.push("`{heading}` differs — the allocations row's is {groupDigits(logged.count())} character(s), this one's is {groupDigits(other.count())}, and they part at character {groupDigits(at)}:\n      allocations: {divergenceExcerpt(logged, at: at)}\n      this table:  {divergenceExcerpt(other, at: at)}")
end 'recordCellDifference'

// The differing cells, one indented paragraph each, with THE TRUNCATION RULE STATED WHERE ITS OUTPUT IS.
// A reader who sees `…` mid-sentence has to know whether they are looking at the whole cell, and the
// answer belongs beside the excerpt rather than in the source of the tool that printed it.
function cellDifferenceReport(differing StringArray) returns String
	return "{indentedList(differing, indent: DifferingCellIndent)}{DifferingCellIndent}(a cell longer than {ExcerptWindowChars} characters is shown as a window of {ExcerptWindowChars} characters around the character they part at; `{OmissionMark}` marks what is cut)"
end 'cellDifferenceReport'

// How deep each differing cell sits under the sentence that introduces it — and the same indent the
// truncation rule itself is printed at, because the rule is a line of that list rather than a footer
// under it.
let DifferingCellIndent = "\n    "

// The character position at which two cells first diverge — or, when one is a prefix of the other, the
// length of the shorter, which is exactly where they part.
//
// CHARACTERS, NOT BYTES. The notes carry `⭐` and `⚠`, and an excerpt cut at a byte offset would slice a
// codepoint in half and print a replacement character where the difference is.
function firstDivergenceAt(logged String, other String) returns GraphemeCount
	var shortest = logged.count()
	if other.count() < shortest 'otherIsShorter'
		shortest = other.count()
	end 'otherIsShorter'

	var loggedIndex = logged.startIndex()
	var otherIndex = other.startIndex()
	var at = 0 as GraphemeCount
	while at < shortest 'walk'
		if not logged.charAt(loggedIndex).equals(other.charAt(otherIndex)) 'diverged'
			return at
		end 'diverged'

		loggedIndex = try logged.indexAfter(loggedIndex) otherwise panic("firstDivergenceAt: at {at} of {shortest} characters, indexAfter failed on the logged cell")
		otherIndex = try other.indexAfter(otherIndex) otherwise panic("firstDivergenceAt: at {at} of {shortest} characters, indexAfter failed on the other cell")
		at = at + 1
	end 'walk'

	return at
end 'firstDivergenceAt'

// A bounded window of `value` around `at` — the ONE place the truncation rule is implemented, and the
// rule `cellDifferenceReport` states.
function divergenceExcerpt(value String, at GraphemeCount) returns String
	let length = value.count()
	if length <= ExcerptWindowChars 'fitsWhole'
		return value
	end 'fitsWhole'

	// Centred on the divergence, then slid back inside the cell: a window hanging off either end would
	// spend its width on nothing, exactly where the interesting text is.
	var start = 0 as GraphemeCount
	if at > ExcerptContextChars 'roomBefore'
		start = at - ExcerptContextChars
	end 'roomBefore'

	if start + ExcerptWindowChars > length 'pastTheEnd'
		start = length - ExcerptWindowChars
	end 'pastTheEnd'

	let stop = start + ExcerptWindowChars
	let window = value.slice(indexAt(value, at: start), endIndex: indexAt(value, at: stop))
	return "{omissionMark(start > 0)}{window}{omissionMark(stop < length)}"
end 'divergenceExcerpt'

// How much of a differing cell the message shows. The window is CENTRED on the divergence, so it is
// twice the context — one fact, not two that could disagree — and forty either side is a terminal line.
let ExcerptContextChars = 40 as GraphemeCount
let ExcerptWindowChars = ExcerptContextChars * 2

// What stands in for the text an excerpt cut. `omissionMark` is what puts it in: empty text for "nothing
// was cut on this side" is this suite's idiom for a message fragment with nothing to say (see
// `LadderIndex.nameListClause`).
let OmissionMark = "…"

function omissionMark(cut bool) returns String
	if not cut 'nothingCut'
		return ""
	end 'nothingCut'

	return OmissionMark
end 'omissionMark'

// The `StringIndex` at character offset `at`. A `StringIndex` carries a BYTE position beside its
// character one, so it cannot be built from an offset — it is walked to. Linear, on a path that runs
// only when a message is being written about a broken document.
function indexAt(value String, at GraphemeCount) returns StringIndex
	var index = value.startIndex()
	var walked = 0 as GraphemeCount
	while walked < at 'walk'
		index = try value.indexAfter(index) otherwise panic("indexAt: walked {walked} of {at} characters into a {value.count()}-character cell and indexAfter failed")
		walked = walked + 1
	end 'walk'

	return index
end 'indexAt'

// The rung columns of a memory row. `AllocCountArray` for both tables: an allocation count and a
// byte volume are the same unsigned counter, and a second array type differing only in its name
// would buy nothing but a cast at every use.
function rungCounts(cells StringArray, source String) returns AllocCountArray throws ScaleError
	var counts = AllocCountArray.create()
	for i in LeadingColumns upto cells.count() 'eachRung'
		counts.push(try parseGroupedCount(try cellAt(cells, index: i, source: source), source: source, column: i))
	end 'eachRung'
	return counts
end 'rungCounts'

// One cell of an already-parsed row, as a THROW rather than a panic: every caller is reading a file a
// human may have edited, so a cell that is not there is bad input, not a bug in this file.
function cellAt(cells StringArray, index ColumnIndex, source String) returns String throws ScaleError
	return try cells.get(index) otherwise throw ScaleError.reason("a row in {source} has {cells.count()} cell(s); column {index} was expected")
end 'cellAt'

// `19,261,978` -> 19261978. The tables carry digit groups because a column of seven-figure numbers is
// unreadable without them, and the reader has to undo exactly what the writer did.
//
// ⚠ **`parseCount` IS THE SUITE'S ONE DECIMAL PARSER AND ITS MESSAGE CANNOT NAME A DOCUMENT** — it is
// shared with the metrics TSV, the `--mm-trace` `size=` fields, `--rungs` and `--repeat`, so a reader of
// a hand-edited trend log was told only `expected a number, got 'oops'`, with nothing saying which file,
// which table or which column. Adding a context parameter THERE would make four other call sites carry a
// sentence two of them discard unread; the caller that knows the document is the one that says so. This
// is that caller, and the column index is what turns "a cell" into a RUNG a reader can go and look at.
function parseGroupedCount(cell String, source String, column ColumnIndex) returns ParsedCount throws ScaleError
	let value = try parseCount(cell.replace(",", with: "")) otherwise (error) 'notACount'
		throw ScaleError.reason("column {column} of a row in {source} is not a count — {scaleErrorText(error)}. Every column past the {LeadingColumns} leading ones is one rung's number, written with digit-group commas.")
	end 'notACount'

	return value
end 'parseGroupedCount'

// ===========================================================================================
// Writing the log
// ===========================================================================================

// Did this run's numbers move against the last logged row?
//
// This is a READING, and nothing branches on it: a `--note` records the run whether it moved or not,
// because a note is an instruction from the operator (see `recordRun`). It decides one sentence of
// prose — whether the run is told that its allocations and bytes are unchanged.
//
// Everything this instrument measures is bit-for-bit reproducible for one checkout, so the same
// compiler on the same corpus produces the same digits and a difference here is a REAL difference in
// what the compiler does. There is nothing else to ask about: the machine-dependent half of the old
// suite, which would have "moved" on every run without the compiler changing at all, is not measured
// any more.
//
// ⚠ THE BYTE COLUMN IS ONLY CONSULTED WHEN IT MAY BE. Across two checkouts it carries a path term that
// GROWS with the program (see the header), so asking it whether the compiler moved would answer "yes"
// for a run that changed nothing — and this reading decides the sentence that tells the operator exactly
// that. When bytes are withheld the question is asked of the allocation column alone.
//
// ⚠ AND THAT IS THE BEST ANSWER AVAILABLE, NOT A CLEAN ONE — this comment said "which is path-independent
// and can answer it" until that was measured and refused. Across two minting paths the allocation column
// carries a CONSTANT offset, so it too can report a compiler that did not move. It is still the column to
// ask: it is the only one left, and what it gets wrong is bounded — a flat per-rung delta may be the path,
// a delta that GROWS per rung cannot be. The report does not resolve that for the reader and must not
// pretend to; `printMintedElsewhere` prints the bound immediately above the sentence this decides.
//
// ⚠ AND IT IS ASKED IN EXACTLY ONE PLACE. The delta report used to accumulate its own `moved` flag from
// its own per-cell comparison while this function answered the same question for the recording sentence,
// so the withheld rule was written twice, in two files, and nothing made the two agree — they already
// disagreed about a run whose ladder is shorter than the log's. One run, one answer.
export function anyMemoryMoved(trend LoggedMemory, results RungResultArray, bytes ByteDelta) returns bool
	return match trend 'm'
		absent gives true
		present(_, _, _, allocs, loggedBytes) gives memoryDiffers(results, loggedAllocs: allocs, loggedBytes: loggedBytes, bytes: bytes)
	end 'm'
end 'anyMemoryMoved'

function memoryDiffers(results RungResultArray, loggedAllocs AllocCountArray, loggedBytes ByteCountArray, bytes ByteDelta) returns bool
	// ONLY THE RUNGS BOTH RUNS CLIMBED WERE COMPARED, and a rung that was not compared cannot have moved
	// — the same argument that withholds the byte column, applied to a rung with no past at all. This used
	// to answer "moved" for a ladder of a different length, which reported a --rungs=3 run against a
	// six-rung log as a movement in a compiler that had not moved.
	var compared = results.count()
	if loggedAllocs.count() < compared 'shorterPast'
		compared = loggedAllocs.count()
	end 'shorterPast'

	for i in 0 upto compared 'eachRung'
		let result = try results.get(i) otherwise panic("memoryDiffers: i < results.count() but results.get({i}) failed")
		let allocs = try loggedAllocs.get(i) otherwise panic("memoryDiffers: i < loggedAllocs.count() but get({i}) failed")
		if result.total.allocs != allocs 'allocsMoved'
			return true
		end 'allocsMoved'

		let logged = try loggedBytes.get(i) otherwise panic("memoryDiffers: the two logged columns are the same length — loadScaleTrend refuses a log where they are not")
		if bytesMoved(result.total.bytes, logged: logged, bytes: bytes) 'bytesMoved'
			return true
		end 'bytesMoved'
	end 'eachRung'

	return false
end 'memoryDiffers'

function bytesMoved(current ByteCount, logged ByteCount, bytes ByteDelta) returns bool
	return match bytes 'b'
		withheld gives false
		comparable gives current != logged
	end 'b'
end 'bytesMoved'

// Refuse a `--note` this run could not honestly record — BEFORE the ladder is climbed, not after.
// Rejecting the command line once the run has already spent thirty seconds compiling six rungs would
// be a rude way to report a typo.
export function assertRecordable(trend LoggedMemory, note String, mintedAt String, rungs RungCount) throws ScaleError
	try assertCellSafe(note, what: "a --note")

	// The minting paths go into a cell of the same row, so they are screened by the same door. A path
	// CAN legally contain a `|` on POSIX, which is exactly the kind of input that would otherwise be
	// trusted because nobody typed it.
	try assertCellSafe(mintedAt, what: "the run's minting path ({mintedAt})")

	match trend 'm'
		absent then return
		present then break 'm'
	end 'm'

	// THE `absent` ARM RETURNED ABOVE, so `loggedRungCount`'s own refusal cannot be raised from here —
	// and that is why this is `otherwise panic` rather than a bare `try` propagating it. The refusal is
	// NOT dead: `loggedRungCount` is an exported member of an exported union, so any future caller may
	// ask it without a guard, and a `RungCount` it could not honestly answer would have to be a sentinel.
	// Saying so at the one site that has already established the arm is what keeps the two facts — the
	// refusal is live, and it is unreachable from here — from having to be re-derived by a reader.
	let logged = try trend.loggedRungCount() otherwise panic("assertRecordable: the trend is `present`, which is the arm loggedRungCount answers from — the `absent` arm returned four lines above")
	if rungs != logged 'differentLadder'
		throw ScaleError.reason("the log's tables have {logged} rung column(s) and this run climbed {rungs}. A row of a different width would make the tables RAGGED, and a rung's column — which is the whole point of reading them downwards — would stop meaning one rung. Re-run with --rungs={logged}.")
	end 'differentLadder'
end 'assertRecordable'

// EVERY value written verbatim into a markdown cell comes through here — the operator's note and the
// compiler path alike.
//
// A `|` would close the cell it sits in and shift every column after it; a newline would end the row
// entirely. Both produce a table that still renders, as the wrong numbers. And neither may forge a
// table marker, which is the anchor every row is inserted above: a cell carrying one would be written
// into the row for the FIRST table and then found by the insert for the SECOND. (`insertRowBefore` is
// the backstop, and it reads the document rather than the cell.)
function assertCellSafe(text String, what String) throws ScaleError
	if text.contains("|") or text.contains("\n") 'wouldBreakTheTable'
		throw ScaleError.reason("{what} may not contain `|` or a newline: the tables in {OptimizationLogRelativePath} are markdown rows, and either would silently shift the columns rather than fail")
	end 'wouldBreakTheTable'

	if text.contains(TableMarkerPrefix) 'wouldForgeAMarker'
		throw ScaleError.reason("{what} may not contain `{TableMarkerPrefix}`: the table markers in {OptimizationLogRelativePath} begin with it, the cell is written verbatim into the row inserted above each of them, and a forged marker would decide where the NEXT row landed")
	end 'wouldForgeAMarker'
end 'assertCellSafe'

// Append this run to both tables.
//
// The file is re-read here rather than carried down from `loadScaleTrend`. It is a document a human
// edits, and the edit that matters most — a paragraph of prose explaining the row about to be
// written — is one they may well make WHILE the six rungs are compiling. Writing back a copy read
// thirty seconds ago would silently discard it.
export function recordScaleRun(results RungResultArray, note String, mintedAt String) throws ScaleError
	// SCREENED HERE, AT THE WRITE, and not only up front. `assertRecordable` asks the same question
	// before the ladder is climbed so that a typo is reported in a second rather than in thirty — but
	// that makes it a COURTESY, and if it were the only check then the guarantee would rest on every
	// future caller remembering to ask. The function that writes the cell is the one that can promise it.
	try assertCellSafe(note, what: "a --note")
	try assertCellSafe(mintedAt, what: "the run's minting path ({mintedAt})")

	let path = try FilePath.from(OptimizationLogRelativePath) otherwise panic("recordScaleRun: '{OptimizationLogRelativePath}' parsed as a path when the trend was loaded")
	let existing = try File.readText(path) otherwise throw ScaleError.reason("could not re-read {OptimizationLogRelativePath}")

	let today = todayUtc()
	var updated = try insertRowBefore(existing, marker: AllocationsTableMarker, row: renderMemoryRow(results, note: note, mintedAt: mintedAt, date: today, measure: ScaleMeasure.allocations), source: OptimizationLogRelativePath)
	updated = try insertRowBefore(updated, marker: BytesTableMarker, row: renderMemoryRow(results, note: note, mintedAt: mintedAt, date: today, measure: ScaleMeasure.bytes), source: OptimizationLogRelativePath)
	updated = try insertRowBefore(updated, marker: CpuTableMarker, row: renderMemoryRow(results, note: note, mintedAt: mintedAt, date: today, measure: ScaleMeasure.cpuTicks), source: OptimizationLogRelativePath)

	try File.writeText(path, content: updated) otherwise throw ScaleError.reason("could not write {OptimizationLogRelativePath}")

	print("\nscale-test: recorded '{note}' ({today}) in {OptimizationLogRelativePath}\n")
end 'recordScaleRun'

// Insert one row immediately above one marker — THE way a row reaches any of the three tables.
//
// THE MARKER MUST APPEAR EXACTLY ONCE, and that is now checked rather than assumed. This was three
// calls to `String.replace`, which replaces EVERY occurrence, against a reader (`readTable`) that
// anchors on the FIRST — so the writer and the reader did not even agree on where a table was, and
// two things fell out of that:
//
//   - A marker that had gone MISSING (the file is a document a human edits, and this re-reads it
//     after the ladder has been climbed) made `replace` a silent no-op. The file was rewritten
//     unchanged and the tool then printed `recorded '<note>'`. A lie, and the kind that is only
//     discovered six months later when the row is not there.
//   - A `--note` carrying a marker's text was written into the row inserted for the FIRST table, and
//     the replacement for the SECOND then matched it — injecting a bytes row into the middle of the
//     allocations table. (`assertRecordable` refuses such a note up front; this is the backstop that
//     makes the document itself, rather than a guess about what a note might contain, the thing that
//     decides.)
//
// It throws BEFORE anything is written — the three inserts all run against a string, and the file is
// written once, at the end — so a log that fails here is left exactly as it was.
//
// EXPORTED, alongside `parseScaleTrend`, and for the same reason: it is a document-to-document function
// whose refusal nothing ran. The other route to it is `recordScaleRun`, which WRITES `docs/optimization-
// log.md` — so a self-test driving that entry point would either edit the project's trend record or
// prove the refusal against a copy, and the second is a parser of the same document in a second place.
// This one takes the text and gives text back, so the refusal is provable without either.
export function insertRowBefore(text String, marker String, row String, source String) returns String throws ScaleError
	let parts = text.split(marker)
	if parts.count() != MarkerSplitParts 'notExactlyOnce'
		throw ScaleError.reason("`{marker}` appears {parts.count() - 1} time(s) in {source} and must appear exactly once — a row is inserted immediately above it, and `readTable` reads the table immediately above it")
	end 'notExactlyOnce'

	let before = try parts.get(0) otherwise panic("insertRowBefore: split gave {MarkerSplitParts} parts but get(0) failed")
	let after = try parts.get(1) otherwise panic("insertRowBefore: split gave {MarkerSplitParts} parts but get(1) failed")
	return "{before}{row}{marker}{after}"
end 'insertRowBefore'

// Splitting on a needle that occurs exactly once yields exactly two parts, one of which may be empty.
let MarkerSplitParts = 2

// One memory row: the date, WHERE IT WAS MEASURED, the reason, then this run's number at every rung.
//
// The provenance is written into all three tables identically, exactly as the date and the note are —
// `readTable` cross-checks the leading cells of the allocations and bytes rows against each other, and a
// path recorded in one table but not the other would say the byte column came from a run the allocation
// column did not.
//
// The rows carry ABSOLUTE numbers, not deltas. A delta answers "did this change help", which the
// report already told you at the moment you ran it; the column answers "where are we now, and how did
// we get here", which is the only question worth keeping a log to answer. Reading a rung's column
// downwards IS the progression.
//
// FREES ARE NOT LOGGED, and their absence is deliberate. They track allocations almost exactly, so a
// third table of them would be a near-duplicate paid for in width. They are measured, reported, and
// carried in `--result-json` for anyone who wants them.
function renderMemoryRow(results RungResultArray, note String, mintedAt String, date String, measure ScaleMeasure) returns String
	var cells = StringArray.create()
	cells.push(date)
	cells.push(mintedAt)
	cells.push(note)

	for result in results 'eachRung'
		cells.push(groupDigits(measureOf(result.total, measure: measure) as Delta))
	end 'eachRung'

	return renderRow(cells)
end 'renderMemoryRow'

function renderRow(cells StringArray) returns String
	var row = StringBuilder.create()
	row.append("|")
	for cell in cells 'each'
		row.append(" {cell} |")
	end 'each'
	row.append("\n")
	return row.build()
end 'renderRow'

// ===========================================================================================
// Formatting
// ===========================================================================================

// 19261978 -> "19,261,978". Six-figure allocation counts stacked in a column are unreadable without
// separators, and these tables exist to be read at a glance.
export function groupDigits(value Delta) returns String
	if value < 0 'negative'
		return "-{groupDigits(-value)}"
	end 'negative'

	let digits = "{value}"
	let count = digits.count()

	var grouped = StringBuilder.create()
	var index = 0 as GroupIndex
	for digit in digits 'eachDigit'
		// A separator goes before every digit whose distance from the END is a multiple of three —
		// except the first, which would otherwise produce a leading comma.
		if index > 0 and (count - index) mod DigitGroupSize == 0 'boundary'
			grouped.append(",")
		end 'boundary'

		grouped.append("{digit}")
		index = index + 1
	end 'eachDigit'

	return grouped.build()
end 'groupDigits'

// ===========================================================================================
// The calendar
// ===========================================================================================

// Today, as YYYY-MM-DD in UTC.
//
// UTC, NOT local time, and that is a real decision rather than laziness: the log is committed and
// read by whoever picks the work up next, and a local date silently means something different
// depending on who ran the command. An entry stamped a day "early" because the machine was in
// Sydney is worse than one stamped in a timezone everyone can agree on.
function todayUtc() returns String
	let days = WallClock.nowUnixSeconds() / SecondsPerDay
	let civil = civilFromDays(days)
	return "{civil.year}-{padTwo(civil.month)}-{padTwo(civil.day)}"
end 'todayUtc'

type CivilDate
	export let year as CivilPart
	export let month as CivilPart
	export let day as CivilPart

	static function create(year CivilPart, month CivilPart, day CivilPart) returns CivilDate
		return Self{year: year, month: month, day: day}
	end 'create'
end 'CivilDate'

// Days-since-the-Unix-epoch to a civil (proleptic Gregorian) date.
//
// This is Howard Hinnant's `civil_from_days`, and the reason it looks like nothing is because it is
// doing something genuinely awkward: the Gregorian calendar's leap rule repeats on a 400-year ERA of
// exactly 146097 days, but the year does not START where that cycle is easiest to count from. The
// trick is to shift the epoch to 0000-03-01 (the +719468) so that the leap day lands at the END of
// the year, which makes February's irregularity fall off the end of the arithmetic instead of
// sitting in the middle of it. March is then month 0, and the last two lines put January and
// February back where humans expect them.
//
// It is branch-free integer arithmetic, exact for every date, and it is not worth re-deriving. See
// https://howardhinnant.github.io/date_algorithms.html#civil_from_days
function civilFromDays(days DayCount) returns CivilDate
	let shifted = days + 719468
	let era = shifted / 146097
	let dayOfEra = shifted - era * 146097
	let yearOfEra = (dayOfEra - dayOfEra / 1460 + dayOfEra / 36524 - dayOfEra / 146096) / 365
	let year = yearOfEra + era * 400
	let dayOfYear = dayOfEra - (365 * yearOfEra + yearOfEra / 4 - yearOfEra / 100)

	// Months counted from March, so a leap day cannot land in the middle of the sequence.
	let marchMonth = (5 * dayOfYear + 2) / 153
	let day = dayOfYear - (153 * marchMonth + 2) / 5 + 1

	var month = marchMonth + 3
	var calendarYear = year
	if marchMonth >= 10 'januaryOrFebruary'
		// March-relative months 10 and 11 are January and February — of the NEXT calendar year.
		month = marchMonth - 9
		calendarYear = year + 1
	end 'januaryOrFebruary'

	return CivilDate.create(calendarYear as CivilPart, month: month, day: day)
end 'civilFromDays'

function padTwo(value CivilPart) returns String
	if value < 10 'single'
		return "0{value}"
	end 'single'

	return "{value}"
end 'padTwo'
